in this lesson we're going to talk about  the types of numbers so the different  types of sets of numbers that there are  and the important thing to understand  here is that sometimes they're going to  mention these in the course of a problem  and they'll say you know is this  solution in this set or is this solution  in that set or they might just make a  reference to a special type of a number  and it's good to just know what these  are so let's just go through them real  quick when they talk about the natural  numbers when you think of a natural  numbers think of like how a child would  would count if you say count to 10 okay  to your  little brother little sister or niece  nephew that kind of thing  they're gonna always start with one  right they're gonna say one two three  four five like that okay they never  start with zero they never mention  negative numbers those these are like  the counting numbers okay starting with  one those are the natural numbers then  we get to the integers now the integers  these are basically round numbers you  don't see any decimals in here you don't  see any fractions they're just whole  numbers they can be positive they can be  negative they can be zero so you can see  these are what integers are okay  and then whole numbers whole numbers are  basically like the natural numbers the  counting numbers with the addition of  zero so it's one two three four five you  don't see any negative values here you  don't see any decimals or fractions  these are whole numbers zero and the  positive integers okay now rational  numbers when they mean when they say  rational is that you can write the  quantity as a ratio meaning a fraction  of an integer divided by an integer so  for example if i have .1.17  okay that can be written as 17 100. 17  is an integer right 100 is an integer  and we're writing as a ratio of two  integers another example would be square  root of  16. well square root of 16 is 4 and 4  can be written as 4 over 1 right  repeating numbers let's say 0.2  repeating that's actually  can be written as 2 over 9. so that's an  integer by an integer  now an irrational number is not a  rational number it can't be written as a  ratio of an integer  over an integer for for example square  roots that you can't simplify like  square root of 17 this comes out to a  non-repeating non-terminating decimal  another example would be something like  pi you know pi is  3.14159 it doesn't repeat and it doesn't  stop you can't write it as a ratio of an  integer over an integer or the natural  the natural number  e okay so that's 2.71 and it doesn't  repeat and doesn't terminate  so that's rational irrational now real  numbers and complex numbers  for most of your math you know learning  so far you've been working with real  numbers uh complex numbers are basically  imaginary numbers okay so it's a  combination of part real plus imaginary  and where the imaginary numbers come in  are where you're taking the square roots  of negative numbers so if you have the  square root of negative 16 that square  root of negative 1 is i the square root  of 16 is 4 and we write it as 4i so if  it's not an imaginary number it falls  into the category of a real number okay  so if you don't see any i's or square  roots of negative numbers it's real  otherwise complex numbers are  combination of part real part imaginary  and for example you could write 3 plus  2i that's a part real part of measure  that's a complex number so  this is just a quick overview of the  different types of numbers that you're  going to encounter on the act and  they're going to make mention of these  in the various problems so  as long as you're familiar with these  that'll make it a lot easier so i'll see  you in the next lesson
 

